      "solution": "$y = \\frac{x^3}{x^2 - 1}$\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{3x^2(x^2 - 1) - (2x)x^3}{(x^2 - 1)^2} \\quad \\{\\text{quotient rule}\\}\n= \\frac{3x^4 - 3x^2 - 2x^4}{(x^2 - 1)^2}\n= \\frac{x^4 - 3x^2}{(x^2 - 1)^2}$\n\nWhen $x = 2$, $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{(2)^4 - 3(2)^2}{(2^2 - 1)^2} = \\frac{16 - 12}{9} = \\frac{4}{9}$.\nSo, the tangent has gradient $\\frac{4}{9}$.",
      "ai_explanation": {
        "concept_rule": "Quotient rule: $\\frac{d}{dx}\\left[\\frac{u}{v}\\right] = \\frac{u'v - uv'}{v^2}$.",
        "step_by_step": [
          "Identify $u = x^3$, $v = x^2 - 1$, so $u' = 3x^2$, $v' = 2x$.",
          "Apply quotient rule: $\\frac{3x^2(x^2 - 1) - x^3(2x)}{(x^2 - 1)^2}$.",
          "Simplify the numerator: $3x^4 - 3x^2 - 2x^4 = x^4 - 3x^2$.",
          "Substitute $x = 2$: $\\frac{2^4 - 3(2^2)}{(2^2 - 1)^2} = \\frac{16 - 12}{3^2} = \\frac{4}{9}$."
        ],
        "common_pitfalls": "Reversing the terms in the numerator of the quotient rule ($uv' - u'v$ instead of $u'v - uv'$)."
